    "result_text": "All tests pass (the acceptance test is the only test class in the suite; `BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the structured `OWNER_CREATED` audit event alongside the existing human-readable audit line, keeping each concern in its own small unit.\n\n**What was added:**\n\n1. **`Owner.getPrimaryIdentifier()`** \u2014 the single source of truth for \"the owner's current primary identifier.\" It returns `customerCode` today; when the customer code is later unified into a member id, only this one method changes and the event follows automatically. This is the key design point the spec calls out.\n\n2. **`OwnerCreatedEvent`** (record) \u2014 the immutable event, whose component order fixes the JSON shape `{seq, ownerId, customerCode, membershipLevel, event}` and pins the `OWNER_CREATED` discriminator.\n\n3. **`OwnerEventPublisher`** (`@Component`) \u2014 owns the monotonic `AtomicLong` sequence (shared across creates) and serialises/logs the event as JSON to the `AUDIT` logger. Stateful, machine-readable concern kept separate from the plain audit line.\n\n4. **`EmitOwnerCreatedEvent`** \u2014 a one-method OfficeFloor function that pulls the saved `@Val Owner` and delegates to the publisher.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 a new `emitEvent` step wired after `audit` and before `respond`, under the same transaction governance as its neighbours.\n\n`AuditOwnerCreated` was left untouched, so the existing line still emits *in addition to* the new event. Emitted output confirmed:\n\n```\nINFO AUDIT - Created owner id=11 customerCode=NSW-16E6609E ... membershipLevel=1 ...\nINFO AUDIT - {\"seq\":1,\"ownerId\":11,\"customerCode\":\"NSW-16E6609E\",\"membershipLevel\":1,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}\n```\n\nThe event carries the primary identifier via `getPrimaryIdentifier()`, so the switch to a future `memberId` is a one-line change with no touch to the event, publisher, function, or wiring.",
